Mild Soft Tissue Contusion
Common Infections and Afflictions
Localized tenderness, Ecchymosis, History of blunt trauma

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-030
Reason for Visit: Acute trauma to the thigh followed by severe swelling and pain.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, high-impact blunt force trauma to the thigh resulting in rapid, focal edema and significant pain upon movement. Given the mechanism of injury (e.g., a fall against furniture or a heavy object strike) and the rapid onset of swelling, an urgent on-site clinical assessment is required to definitively rule out a femoral fracture, an acute muscle tear, or an evolving, limb-threatening acute compartment syndrome. Delaying this structured physical evaluation poses an unacceptable risk of missing a surgical emergency or an occult fracture, rigorously fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I smashed my leg into the edge of the heavy oak table, and it immediately swelled up into a hard, painful lump.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 3 hours ago following a direct blunt force trauma (colliding heavily with a solid wooden table edge). The patient reports immediate localized pain and the rapid development of focal edema and swelling directly over the impact site on the anterior thigh. The pain is described as a deep ache, distinctly exacerbated by direct pressure or movement of the underlying quadriceps muscle. A rapidly developing ecchymosis is present, currently appearing red-purple. The patient notes the overlying epidermis is completely intact with no lacerations. The patient has retained full weight-bearing capability. Denies distal numbness, tingling, or "pins and needles."
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a pronounced limp due to pain upon knee flexion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Minor fall risk due to antalgic gait.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 128/84 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Guarding the affected limb.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A negative plain radiograph ruling out underlying fracture would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but is not clinically indicated given the current physical findings.
Physical Exam: Right anterior thigh reveals a distinct, 6cm x 5cm area of firm focal edema and swelling directly corresponding to the reported impact site. Significant localized point tenderness is present over the swelling. An early red-purple ecchymosis is visible. The overlying epidermis is definitively intact. Crucially, there is an absolute absence of palpable bony step-off, crepitus, or deformity along the length of the femur. Active and passive range of motion of the knee and hip joints are normal, though knee flexion past 90 degrees elicits sharp muscular pain. The thigh compartments are soft, non-tense, and compressible. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are bounding. Capillary refill is <2 seconds. Neurovascular exam is entirely normal.
Environmental Observations: The offending table is visible; no other trip hazards noted.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient applied a frozen gel pack for 20 minutes prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Mild Soft Tissue Contusion - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Femoral Shaft/Distal Fracture - Definitively ruled out by the lack of bony deformity, absence of severe resting pain, intact ability to fully bear weight, and full active range of motion of the adjacent joints.
Acute Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out clinically as the affected muscle compartment is soft and compressible (not tense/woody), and there is no pain out of proportion to passive stretching of the quadriceps.
Quadriceps Muscle Rupture - Unlikely given the lack of a palpable gap or defect in the muscle belly, and the intact active extension of the knee against gravity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of rapidly developing, painful thigh swelling following a high-impact trauma required an urgent home evaluation to physically clear the limb of a fracture or compartment syndrome. Direct, structured clinical examination confirmed an isolated, uncomplicated soft tissue contusion (hematoma) with intact structural and vascular integrity. By confirming the benign nature of the injury and instituting specific conservative management, an unnecessary, painful, and distressing transport to a hospital emergency department for x-rays was successfully averted. This necessary clinical triage safely managed the acute episode in the home, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Advised regular Paracetamol 1000mg up to four times daily. Recommended Ibuprofen 400mg strictly with food for 3-5 days to manage the inflammatory response. Instructed continued strict adherence to the RICE protocol (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation) for the next 48 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: To minimize the size of the internal bruise and speed up your recovery, firmly wrap the thigh in an elastic compression bandage starting just below the knee and working upward. This physical pressure acts like a tourniquet on the bleeding capillaries inside the muscle, forcing the bleeding to stop and preventing the blood from pooling into a large, painful clot.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Tubular compression bandage (Tubigrip) applied during the consultation.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 7-10 days if the swelling does not significantly decrease or if a hard, painful lump persists in the muscle.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if your entire leg becomes swollen, rock-hard, and excruciatingly painful, if your foot turns cold and pale, or if you become unable to bear weight.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; a deep hematoma can occasionally calcify (myositis ossificans) if not rehabilitated correctly.
Clinical Course: The acute swelling and pain will peak within 24-48 hours. The ecchymosis will spectacularly change color from purple to green to yellow over the next 10-14 days as the body reabsorbs the trapped blood. Full recovery takes 2 to 4 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical attention if the bruise begins to rapidly expand beyond the initial impact zone, or if the pain dramatically worsens despite regular pain relief.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The blunt force broke hundreds of tiny blood vessels deep inside your muscle. The swelling and pain are caused by this trapped blood irritating the muscle fibers.
Actionable Steps: Do not aggressively massage or apply deep heat to the injured area for the first 72 hours; heat and massage will physically force the broken vessels to reopen and bleed more into the muscle. Stick to gentle stretching and light walking as pain allows.

